### 1. Task outcome (short version):
One double-blind RCT in Zambia [1] shows daily cotrimoxazole prophylaxis reduces all-cause mortality in HIV-infected 
children (HR 0.57, 95% CI 0.43–0.77), with similar serious adverse event rates and a borderline increase in severe 
neutropenia. Illness episode details and hospitalizations were not reported in the abstract; antimicrobial resistance 
outcomes were not assessed.

### 2. Task outcome (extremely detailed version):
- PICO: HIV-infected children (1–14 years) in Zambia; cotrimoxazole prophylaxis vs placebo; median 19-month follow-up; 
resource-poor setting with high bacterial resistance [1].
- Mortality: HR 0.57 (95% CI 0.43–0.77; p=0.0002); 28% vs 42% deaths (cotrimoxazole vs placebo). Effect persisted beyond
12 months (HR 0.48, 95% CI 0.27–0.84) [1].
- Illness episodes: PCP rarely detected (1 case, placebo arm); no detailed counts for other infections or 
hospitalizations in abstract [1].
- Safety: Grade 3/4 AEs similar (6% vs 7%); severe neutropenia higher with cotrimoxazole (6% vs 3%; p=0.06) [1].
- Risk of bias: Randomized, double-blind, ITT; allocation concealment unclear; early stopping; incomplete reporting of 
attrition and morbidity outcomes (overall moderate quality; risk of bias unclear) [1].
- Applicability: Direct to HIV-infected children in low-resource, high-resistance settings; uncertain for infants of 
HIV-infected mothers [1].
- Conclusions: Moderate-strength evidence supports routine cotrimoxazole prophylaxis to reduce mortality in HIV-infected
children; more research needed on specific morbidity, hospitalizations, and antimicrobial resistance.

### 3. Additional context (if relevant):
Despite high background resistance, mortality benefits were observed, suggesting mechanisms beyond direct pathogen 
susceptibility may contribute (e.g., prevention of a subset of bacterial infections or immunomodulatory effects). 
However, resistance emergence during prophylaxis was not evaluated; future trials should include resistance surveillance
and standardized morbidity/hospitalization endpoints.
