Final answer: # Systematic Review Report

## 1. Review Summary
- **Title:** Cotrimoxazole prophylaxis for opportunistic infections in children with HIV infection
- **Objective:** To assess the effects of routinely administered cotrimoxazole on death and illness episodes in children
with HIV infection, and in infants of HIV infected mothers.

## 2. Search Results
- Number of candidate studies found: 10

## 3. Screening Results
- Included studies: 1
- Excluded studies: 9
- Decisions:
  - Study 1: INCLUDE — Double-blind randomized placebo-controlled trial of routine co-trimoxazole prophylaxis in 
HIV-infected children aged 1–14 years; comparator is placebo; outcomes include mortality. Meets all selection criteria.
  - Study 2: EXCLUDE — Randomized trial but exclusively in adults; population does not meet child/infant criteria.
  - Study 3: EXCLUDE — Randomized/no-therapy comparison but conducted in adults with AIDS (Kaposi's sarcoma); population
not children/infants.
  - Study 4: EXCLUDE — Case series in adult men; not randomized; not a prophylaxis trial.
  - Study 5: EXCLUDE — Observational postmortem study in children; no randomized comparison of cotrimoxazole 
prophylaxis.
  - Study 6: EXCLUDE — Prospective cohort of HIV-exposed/infected children; no randomized prophylaxis intervention.
  - Study 7: EXCLUDE — Prospective observational study; not a randomized or quasi-randomized trial of cotrimoxazole.
  - Study 8: EXCLUDE — Randomized trial but population is adults with tuberculosis; not children/infants.
  - Study 9: EXCLUDE — Narrative/review on mother-to-child transmission; not a randomized prophylaxis trial.
  - Study 10: EXCLUDE — Diagnostic/epidemiologic study in HIV-infected children; prophylaxis status observational; no 
randomized comparison.

## 4. Evidence Extraction Summary
**Study 1** — CHAP trial, Zambia (year not stated in abstract)
- **Population:** Children aged 1–14 years with clinical features of HIV infection; 534 HIV-positive participants after 
excluding 7 HIV-negative; resource-poor setting with high bacterial resistance.
- **Setting:** Zambia; resource-poor setting with high local bacterial resistance to cotrimoxazole.
- **Intervention:** Daily co-trimoxazole (trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole) prophylaxis; dosing regimen not specified in 
abstract; initiated at enrollment and continued during follow-up.
- **Comparator:** Placebo (double-blind, randomized).
- **Outcomes:**
  - All-cause mortality (overall): HR 0.57 (95% CI 0.43–0.77); p=0.0002
  - All-cause mortality (subset with >12 months follow-up): HR 0.48 (95% CI 0.27–0.84)
  - Grade 3/4 adverse events (overall incidence comparison)
  - Severe neutropenia (<0.5 × 10^9/L) at any time; p=0.06
  - PCP detected among pneumonia cases (nasopharyngeal aspirates)
- **Adverse events:**
  - Grade 3/4 adverse events: 16/??? (6%) co-trimoxazole vs 18/??? (7%) placebo (overall similar).
  - Rash: 1 case (placebo).
  - Severe neutropenia (<0.5 × 10^9/L): 16/??? (6%) co-trimoxazole vs 7/??? (3%) placebo; p=0.06 (borderline 
difference).
- **Study design:** RCT
- **Follow-up:** Median 19 months; data and safety monitoring committee recommended early stopping in Oct 2003. Analyses
by intention to treat. Also reported subset with >12 months of follow-up.
- **Key findings:**
  - Mortality reduced with daily cotrimoxazole vs placebo: HR 0.57 (95% CI 0.43–0.77; p=0.0002).
  - Benefit consistent across ages (heterogeneity p=0.82) and baseline CD4 strata (heterogeneity p=0.36).
  - Effect persisting in those followed >12 months: HR 0.48 (95% CI 0.27–0.84); heterogeneity vs shorter follow-up 
p=0.60.
  - Deaths: 74 (28%) in cotrimoxazole group vs 112 (42%) in placebo group after median 19 months.
  - Overall grade 3/4 adverse event rates similar between groups (6% vs 7%).
  - Severe neutropenia was somewhat more frequent with cotrimoxazole (6% vs 3%; p=0.06).
  - PCP was rarely identified: 1 case detected (placebo) among 73 nasopharyngeal aspirates from pneumonia cases.
- **Notes:** Double-blind randomized placebo-controlled trial in Zambia with high background bacterial resistance to 
cotrimoxazole. Dosing schedule not specified in abstract. Allocation concealment details not stated. Trial stopped early
by DSMB, which may influence effect size estimates. Hospitalizations and specific illness episode counts (e.g., 
pneumonia, diarrhea, malaria) not provided in the abstract; PCP identification was rare. Exact denominators per arm 
after excluding HIV-negative participants not reported in the abstract.


## 5. Synthesis & Conclusions
Population, setting, and PICO: One randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial in Zambia enrolled children aged 
1–14 years with clinical features of HIV infection (n=534 HIV-positive after exclusions) in a resource-poor setting with
high background bacterial resistance to cotrimoxazole [1]. Participants were randomized to daily cotrimoxazole 
prophylaxis versus placebo. Median follow-up was 19 months, with intention-to-treat analyses; the trial was stopped 
early on DSMB recommendation.

Primary outcomes—mortality and illness episodes: Cotrimoxazole significantly reduced all-cause mortality compared with 
placebo (HR 0.57, 95% CI 0.43–0.77; p=0.0002) [1]. Absolute event rates reported in the abstract were 28% (74 deaths) in
the cotrimoxazole arm versus 42% (112 deaths) in the placebo arm over a median of 19 months, indicating a clinically 
large mortality benefit. Among participants followed >12 months, the mortality effect persisted (HR 0.48, 95% CI 
0.27–0.84) [1]. Effects were consistent across age groups (heterogeneity p=0.82) and baseline CD4 strata (heterogeneity 
p=0.36), suggesting robustness across baseline risk subgroups [1]. Regarding illness episodes, the abstract reported 
very low identification of Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PCP): only one detected case in the placebo arm among 73 
nasopharyngeal aspirates obtained from pneumonia cases [1]. Detailed counts for other opportunistic or common infections
(e.g., pneumonia, diarrhea, malaria) and hospitalizations were not reported in the abstract.

Adverse events and harms: Overall grade 3/4 adverse event rates were similar between arms (6% vs 7%) [1]. Severe 
neutropenia (<0.5 × 10^9/L) occurred more often in the cotrimoxazole arm (6% vs 3%), with a borderline p-value (p=0.06) 
[1]. No clear signal for other serious toxicities emerged in the abstract. The trial occurred in a setting with high 
background bacterial resistance to cotrimoxazole, yet still showed mortality benefit; however, emergence or selection of
antimicrobial resistance during prophylaxis was not directly assessed or reported [1].

Heterogeneity and applicability: As only one trial was included, between-study heterogeneity cannot be assessed. 
Within-trial subgroup analyses suggested consistent effects across age and CD4 strata [1]. The setting was a 
low-resource context with high bacterial resistance, making the findings highly applicable to similar environments where
HIV-infected children face high burdens of bacterial and opportunistic infections. The study directly addresses routine 
cotrimoxazole prophylaxis in HIV-infected children; it does not provide evidence for infants of HIV-infected mothers who
are HIV-uninfected, so applicability to that subgroup remains uncertain.

Internal validity considerations: Strengths include randomization, double-blinding, placebo control, and ITT analyses 
[1]. Important limitations include early stopping (which can inflate effect sizes), unclear allocation concealment, and 
incomplete reporting of attrition and illness/hospitalization outcomes in the abstract [1]. On balance, risk of bias is 
judged as unclear to moderate.

Conclusions and implications: Moderate-certainty evidence from one RCT in a high-burden, resource-limited setting 
indicates that routine daily cotrimoxazole prophylaxis substantially reduces all-cause mortality in HIV-infected 
children [1]. Serious adverse events were comparable between groups, with a borderline increase in severe neutropenia. 
Data on specific illness episodes, hospitalizations, and antimicrobial resistance were limited or not reported. Routine 
cotrimoxazole prophylaxis appears justified for HIV-infected children in similar settings, while additional research 
should clarify effects on specific morbidity outcomes, hospitalization rates, resistance patterns, and the net benefit 
in infants of HIV-infected mothers.

## 6. Quality Assessment
- **Study 1:** Quality rating — moderate
  - Strength: Randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled design (reduces performance and detection bias).
  - Strength: Intention-to-treat analysis reported.
  - Strength: Clinically important primary outcome (all-cause mortality) with large, precise effect size (HR 0.57, 95% 
CI 0.43–0.77).
  - Strength: Effects consistent across age and baseline CD4 strata (no subgroup heterogeneity reported).
  - Strength: Conducted in a resource-poor setting with high background bacterial resistance, enhancing relevance to 
similar contexts.
  - Limitation: Allocation concealment details not described in the abstract.
  - Limitation: Trial stopped early by the DSMB, which may overestimate treatment effects.
  - Limitation: Incomplete reporting of denominators and dosing regimen in the abstract.
  - Limitation: Limited reporting on illness episodes (e.g., pneumonia, diarrhea, malaria) and no hospitalization 
outcomes in the abstract.
  - Limitation: Attrition details and handling of missing data not explicitly described in the abstract.
  - Limitation: Selective outcome reporting cannot be excluded from abstract alone.
  - Limitation: Borderline higher severe neutropenia in the cotrimoxazole arm (6% vs 3%; p=0.06).
  - Limitation: No explicit assessment of antimicrobial resistance emergence reported.
  - Bias concern: Randomization process described but allocation concealment unclear.
  - Bias concern: Early stopping may introduce bias toward larger effects.
  - Bias concern: Attrition and selective reporting domains unclear due to limited reporting in abstract.
  - Bias concern: Outcome measurement likely blinded (double-blind), reducing detection bias.
  - Applicability: direct

**Overall evidence strength:** moderate

**Key limitations:**
- Single RCT constitutes the evidence base; no replication across settings or time.
- Early stopping may overestimate effect size.
- Incomplete reporting in the abstract for allocation concealment, attrition, and illness/hospitalization outcomes.
- No direct assessment of antimicrobial resistance emergence.
- No included evidence for infants of HIV-infected mothers who are HIV-uninfected.
